.TH OPENTELEMETRY-JMX-SCRAPER 8 "@DATE@" "opentelemetry-jmx-scraper @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
opentelemetry-jmx-scraper \- collect JVM metrics over JMX and export them via OTLP
.SH DESCRIPTION
The OpenTelemetry JMX scraper connects to a configured JMX endpoint and exports metrics through OTLP.
It runs as the systemd service opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service.
.PP
The scraper needs a Java runtime, which this package does not install.
The runtime is a suggested dependency rather than a required one, so package managers do not pull it in automatically.
Install
.B default-jre
on Debian and Ubuntu, or
.B java-headless
on RPM-based distributions.
.PP
The service unit resolves
.B java
through the PATH of the service, so whichever JVM is first on that PATH is used.
Without one, the service fails to start with "env: java: No such file or directory".
.SH CONFIGURATION
Set otel.jmx.service.url and otel.jmx.target.system in /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties before starting the service.
The service reads the optional environment overrides from /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/jmx-scraper.env.
.TP
.B otel.jmx.service.url
The JMX endpoint to connect to, for example service:jmx:rmi:///jndi/rmi://localhost:9010/jmxrmi.
.TP
.B otel.jmx.target.system
The metric definitions to apply, as one or more comma-separated values from:
.RS
.\" PD 0 sets the inter-paragraph distance to zero, which keeps the bullets
.\" below on consecutive lines instead of double-spaced. The bare .PD after
.\" the list restores the default.
.PD 0
.\" BEGIN GENERATED target systems (make update-jmx-target-systems)
.IP \(bu 2
activemq
.IP \(bu 2
camel
.IP \(bu 2
cassandra
.IP \(bu 2
experimental-cassandra
.IP \(bu 2
experimental-kafka-connect
.IP \(bu 2
hadoop
.IP \(bu 2
hbase
.IP \(bu 2
jetty
.IP \(bu 2
jvm
.IP \(bu 2
kafka
.IP \(bu 2
kafka-consumer
.IP \(bu 2
kafka-producer
.IP \(bu 2
solr
.IP \(bu 2
tomcat
.IP \(bu 2
wildfly
.\" END GENERATED
.PD
.PP
For example, "jvm,tomcat" collects the JVM metrics alongside the Tomcat ones.
That list is what the packaged scraper accepts; see
.UR https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-contrib/tree/main/jmx-scraper
.UE
for what each target system collects.
.RE
.TP
.B otel.jmx.remote.registry.ssl
Set this when the target JVM runs with
com.sun.management.jmxremote.registry.ssl=true.
.PP
Credentials are configured through the environment rather than this file; see
ENVIRONMENT and SECURITY.
.PP
The file also accepts the OpenTelemetry Java SDK's autoconfiguration
properties, so otel.exporter.otlp.protocol, otel.exporter.otlp.headers,
otel.service.name, and otel.metric.export.interval all work here.
See
.UR https://opentelemetry.io/docs/languages/java/configuration/
.UE
for that set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check that the configured endpoint is reachable before enabling the service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
java -jar /usr/lib/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.jar \e
    -test -config /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command connects, prints "JMX connection test OK" or "JMX connection test ERROR", and exits without scraping or exporting anything.
Run it after every change to config.properties.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/config.properties
JMX scraper and OTLP exporter configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/opentelemetry/jmx-scraper/jmx-scraper.env
Environment overrides for the systemd service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.jar
The scraper application.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service
The systemd service unit.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The scraper logs to the journal, which is the only place its errors are visible:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl -u opentelemetry-jmx-scraper.service -f
.fi
.RE
.PP
A rejected configuration exits 1 and a missing Java runtime exits 127.
The unit sets Restart=on-failure, so systemd restarts the service every five seconds instead of leaving it in the failed state.
.BR systemctl (1)
therefore reports a misconfigured service as activating rather than failed.
.PP
An unreachable JMX endpoint is not a startup failure.
The scraper keeps running, logs "Failed to establish JMX connection", and retries until the endpoint answers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR java (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR journalctl (1)
